+**The plumbing already exists — part 1 is narrower than "get a token" sounds.**
+Apps are launched from workflows here, and the mechanism is
+`.github/actions/broker-gh-token`: the job mints a GitHub Actions OIDC JWT and
+exchanges it at the `cf-token-broker` for a short-lived App **installation**
+token. No App private key sits in any repo — the keys live only in the broker,
+which is the ocap distinction §4 already names, implemented. Seven workflows in
+`.github` already call it (`org-defaults`, `registry-graph`, `branch-sweep`,
+`claim-ticket`, `front-desk-add`, `release-cut`, `sign-branch`), so this lane
+adopts an established door rather than opening a new one. What is actually a
+`[settings]` action is *registering the App and its broker entry* — not building
+token plumbing.
+
+**`registry-graph.yml` is the template, and it covers parts 1 and 2 at once.**
+It is the closest sibling to `registry-refresh.yml` and already does the whole
+shape:
+
+```yaml
+permissions: { contents: write, pull-requests: write, id-token: write }
+...
+  uses: ./.github/actions/broker-gh-token
+  with:
+    app: front-desk-registry          # its own pinned entry, not a fan-in one
+    broker-url: ${{ vars.CF_BROKER_URL }}
+    require: contents, pull_requests  # BOTH — see #87
+```
+
+then commits through `createCommitOnBranch` with that token. Three things that
+bite in that order: the job needs `id-token: write` or the OIDC vars are never
+injected; `require:` must name **every** scope the job will use, because the
+partial preflight is what killed the `pin` job at `exit 128` (#87); and
+`broker-url` is `vars.CF_BROKER_URL`, not a secret — an unset *variable* fails
+open, so a typo there does not go red, the step just skips forever.
+
+**Why this is what unblocks the merge, not merely a nicer token.** The blocker
+above is that GitHub starts no workflow runs from `GITHUB_TOKEN` events, so
+`gate` never exists to pass or fail. An App installation token is not
+`GITHUB_TOKEN`: PRs opened with it *do* start runs, so `gate` comes into
+existence and the ruleset has something to evaluate. Part 2 makes the commits
+signed; part 1 is what makes the PR checkable at all. Neither substitutes for the
+other, and part 2 shipping alone leaves the lane exactly as unmergeable as
+before.
+
 **Say this plainly in your PR:** part 2 alone makes the commits signed and the
 PRs still unmergeable. A PR that implies the lane is fixed is worse than one that
 says what remains.
